WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.367 --> 00:00:03.067
-The one and only
Lin-Manuel Miranda is here!

00:00:03.067 --> 00:00:05.067
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:05.067 --> 00:00:09.734
We are talking about
your new movie,

00:00:09.734 --> 00:00:12.667
"Tick, Tick... Boom!",
which I just loved.

00:00:12.667 --> 00:00:14.300
-Thank you.
-I loved it so much.

00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:16.734
I loved every detail
in this thing.

00:00:16.734 --> 00:00:20.334
And I love that story of
struggle.

00:00:20.334 --> 00:00:22.868
And also, are we doing what

00:00:22.868 --> 00:00:24.667
we're doing with our time
on Earth?

00:00:24.667 --> 00:00:27.767
Are we spending it
how we should?

00:00:27.767 --> 00:00:29.868
And are we doing, you know --
It's all that stuff

00:00:29.868 --> 00:00:31.434
and all these dreams.

00:00:31.434 --> 00:00:32.834
And for the most part,

00:00:32.834 --> 00:00:34.834
you don't know if it's going to
work or not.

00:00:34.834 --> 00:00:38.601
The heartbreaking thing about
this is Jonathan Larson died

00:00:38.601 --> 00:00:41.033
before the off-Broadway
performance.

00:00:41.033 --> 00:00:42.567
He never got to see it.

00:00:42.567 --> 00:00:45.367
-Yeah, he died before the first
preview of "Rent" off-Broadway.

00:00:45.367 --> 00:00:51.934
-It's an insane, powerful story,
but you did it so well.

00:00:51.934 --> 00:00:55.400
And I feel like only you can do
this, in a weird way.

00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:57.801
I feel like, this is --
I'm watching it.

00:00:57.801 --> 00:01:00.334
I'm going like,
"Oh, that must have been you."

00:01:00.334 --> 00:01:03.000
I feel like when I saw
Andrew Garfield sleeping with

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:05.234
his Casio keyboard,
that you've done that.

00:01:05.234 --> 00:01:09.167
-Yeah, well, the first time
I had a rough cut of the movie,

00:01:09.167 --> 00:01:11.701
I called my -- one of my best
friends, collaborator,

00:01:11.701 --> 00:01:13.367
Quiara, who wrote
"In the Heights" with me.

00:01:13.367 --> 00:01:15.300
And she lives on my block
in Washington Heights.

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:16.868
And I called her and I said,
"I have a rough cut,"

00:01:16.868 --> 00:01:18.467
and I will love her forever
for this.

00:01:18.467 --> 00:01:20.601
She said, "I'm on my way!"
-Yep.

00:01:20.601 --> 00:01:22.267
-And, like, ran over
to watch it.

00:01:22.267 --> 00:01:24.834
And in the first shot of
Jonathan's apartment --

00:01:24.834 --> 00:01:27.634
and again, I'm making every
decision through the filter

00:01:27.634 --> 00:01:30.033
of "What would
Jonathan Larson do?"

00:01:30.033 --> 00:01:32.834
It's a really faithful
re-creation of his apartment,

00:01:32.834 --> 00:01:36.434
his life, his world, you know,
New York in 1990.

00:01:36.434 --> 00:01:37.968
But they cut to
John's apartment,

00:01:37.968 --> 00:01:39.634
and Quiara turns to me and goes,

00:01:39.634 --> 00:01:41.968
"Lin, that's your bedroom, dude.

00:01:41.968 --> 00:01:44.267
Like, the futon on the floor
and the Casio next to it.

00:01:44.267 --> 00:01:47.601
That's exactly the hovel
you lived in when we met

00:01:47.601 --> 00:01:49.133
and you were 24 years old."

00:01:49.133 --> 00:01:51.000
-And you're writing songs,
and you're like on the keyboard,

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:52.534
and you go,
"I'm not getting anything,

00:01:52.534 --> 00:01:53.734
and I'm figuring this out,"

00:01:53.734 --> 00:01:55.234
and who knows if anyone's
even going to hear it.

00:01:55.234 --> 00:01:58.300
"But I'm writing 'Hamilton'
right now.

00:01:58.300 --> 00:01:59.901
Who knows what
it's going to be?"

00:01:59.901 --> 00:02:03.300
But until --
Andrew Garfield is un--

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:04.534
-Is outrageous.

00:02:04.534 --> 00:02:06.434
-Unbelievable in this.
-Yeah.

00:02:06.434 --> 00:02:07.868
-And I go -- I just --

00:02:07.868 --> 00:02:10.300
Did you know that
Andrew Garfield was your guy?

00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:11.634
-I knew he was my guy.

00:02:11.634 --> 00:02:13.801
I didn't know if he could sing.
-He can.

00:02:13.801 --> 00:02:16.300
-Which is kind of, you know,
a big thing in a musical.

00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:17.734
[ Laughter ]

00:02:17.734 --> 00:02:19.133
-Yeah, you kind of want to
research that before you do it.

00:02:19.133 --> 00:02:21.400
-Yeah, but I saw him
in "Angels in America."

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:23.801
I knew I needed a theater beast
to play Jonathan

00:02:23.801 --> 00:02:26.701
'cause Jonathan Larson
lived and breathed the theater.

00:02:26.701 --> 00:02:29.834
And it needed to be someone who
could sort of act

00:02:29.834 --> 00:02:32.367
all the small, heartbreaking
moments in Jonathan's life

00:02:32.367 --> 00:02:35.667
and someone who could,
like, play a one-man show

00:02:35.667 --> 00:02:38.234
single-handedly
and play to the rafters.

00:02:38.234 --> 00:02:40.367
And I watched Andrew
in "Angels in America."

00:02:40.367 --> 00:02:42.734
It's an eight-hour play,
in two parts.

00:02:42.734 --> 00:02:45.234
You eat dinner at the theater.
-Yeah.

00:02:45.234 --> 00:02:48.534
-And his, like, rib cage
was cracked open.

00:02:48.534 --> 00:02:50.601
He was so incredible.
I just left being like,

00:02:50.601 --> 00:02:53.801
"Well, that guy can do anything.
Like, he can just do anything."

00:02:53.801 --> 00:02:55.868
And so he was my guy.
-He crushed it, man.

00:02:55.868 --> 00:02:58.501
He crushed it.
And then I'm watching the movie.

00:02:58.501 --> 00:03:01.901
I'm loving it, and then who do
I see but my best buddy,

00:03:01.901 --> 00:03:03.367
Tariq Trotter, in the movie.

00:03:03.367 --> 00:03:04.734
-Yeah.
-As a cameo.

00:03:04.734 --> 00:03:06.000
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:09.367
-And I go, "oh, my --"

00:03:09.367 --> 00:03:10.968
And you shot it so well.
-Thank you.

00:03:10.968 --> 00:03:14.267
-The video camera thing --
-That's my happy place.

00:03:14.267 --> 00:03:17.534
1990 is like when I was running
around New York with a camcorder

00:03:17.534 --> 00:03:19.701
wanting to make movies.
-Yeah.

00:03:19.701 --> 00:03:24.467
-And so, to set this up,
the conceit of the movie is

00:03:24.467 --> 00:03:26.601
any time John starts singing,

00:03:26.601 --> 00:03:28.901
we go into the world according
to Jonathan Larson.

00:03:28.901 --> 00:03:32.567
And Jonathan Larson wrote this
rap song called "Play Game"

00:03:32.567 --> 00:03:34.334
in 1990, and it was all about

00:03:34.334 --> 00:03:36.667
how much he didn't like
what was on Broadway.

00:03:36.667 --> 00:03:39.234
It was just, like, he was
deeply ambivalent about it.

00:03:39.234 --> 00:03:42.567
He thought it was really crass
and all really commercial.

00:03:42.567 --> 00:03:44.901
And he was also like,
"And why do I care so much

00:03:44.901 --> 00:03:47.300
about making stuff for it?"

00:03:47.300 --> 00:03:50.501
And when you watch Jonathan do
it, it's pretty lame.

00:03:50.501 --> 00:03:52.534
He's got a sideways pink hat.

00:03:52.534 --> 00:03:55.133
It's very like Garfield rapping.

00:03:55.133 --> 00:03:57.334
It's very like 1990.

00:03:57.334 --> 00:04:00.133
And so I was like,
"Well, in his mind,

00:04:00.133 --> 00:04:01.801
he's the greatest rapper alive,"

00:04:01.801 --> 00:04:04.100
so I cast one of the greatest
rappers alive to do the song.

00:04:04.100 --> 00:04:07.400
-Wow.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:07.400 --> 00:04:10.601
-And I got to shoot,
like, a 1990 music video.

00:04:10.601 --> 00:04:12.334
-Oh, dude, it's so good.
-Yeah.

00:04:12.334 --> 00:04:15.567
-It made me laugh.
I was like, it's so, so fun.

00:04:15.567 --> 00:04:19.000
But I will say congrats on this,
your directorial debut.

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:20.801
-Thank you.
-You hit a home run, buddy.

00:04:20.801 --> 00:04:22.601
-Thank you.
-I want to show a clip.

00:04:22.601 --> 00:04:27.734
Here is Tariq Trotter in "Tick,
Tick... Boom!" Take a look.

00:04:27.734 --> 00:04:29.801
-♪ Walk through Times Square
and what do you see? ♪

00:04:29.801 --> 00:04:32.167
♪ Ugliness where architecture
used to be ♪

00:04:32.167 --> 00:04:34.367
♪ The glamour and style have
been replaced by gaud ♪

00:04:34.367 --> 00:04:36.868
♪ Like the $60,000 spectacle,
it's all a fraud ♪

00:04:36.868 --> 00:04:38.033
♪ That's the play game ♪

00:04:38.033 --> 00:04:39.334
♪ -That's the play game ♪

00:04:39.334 --> 00:04:41.534
♪ -So why do I want to
play the play game? ♪

00:04:41.534 --> 00:04:42.667
♪ That's the play game ♪

00:04:42.667 --> 00:04:43.934
♪ -That's the play game ♪

00:04:43.934 --> 00:04:46.100
♪ -I must be insane
to play the play game ♪

00:04:46.100 --> 00:04:48.234
♪ Even off-Broadway
it's no guarantee ♪

00:04:48.234 --> 00:04:50.801
♪ That some MBA won't decide
what you see ♪

00:04:50.801 --> 00:04:53.200
♪ Just like America lacking
innovation ♪

00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:55.567
♪ Just getting by
on glitz and reputation ♪

00:04:55.567 --> 00:04:57.634
♪ Just like America
on the decline ♪

00:04:57.634 --> 00:05:00.467
♪ Unconcerned with the product,
just the bottom line ♪

00:05:00.467 --> 00:05:04.234
-That's exactly --
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:04.234 --> 00:05:06.467
That is how you do it
right there.

00:05:06.467 --> 00:05:08.300
That is how you do it, buddy.

00:05:08.300 --> 00:05:10.234
I love you. Lin-Manuel Miranda.

00:05:10.234 --> 00:05:12.734
"Tick, Tick... Boom!"
is in theaters now

00:05:12.734 --> 00:05:14.300
and on Netflix this Friday.

